      "year": 2020,
      "abstract": "Abstract:Charles Baudelaire\u2019s 1853 essay, \u201cMorale du joujou,\u201d draws on a childhood memory in an analysis of play that celebrates the beautiful and bizarre in toys as art and children as proto-artists. But it is also a critique of economic inequities that impact children and includes the story of the poor boy\u2019s toy that reappears later in a prose poem of that title.This essay proposes to read \u201cMorale du joujou\u201d within Walter Benjamin\u2019s historical materialist model as a way to untangle its seeming contradictions. The narrator\u2019s recollection, this moment of return to childhood, bears a resemblance to the historian\u2019s task for Benjamin;it depends on an intense cathetic relationship as a means of upsetting the linear temporality underlying conventional historiography. It may be read as an instance of Benjamin\u2019s critical historical concept, a dialectical image, the threshold of awakening from the phantasmagorical dream of the nineteenth century, just as child\u2019s play is a model for Benjamin\u2019s dialectician who collects fragments.In other words,Baudelaire\u2019s childhood memory gains meaning in light of Benjamin\u2019s \u201cnow of recognizability,\u201d revealing a potential for economic and political disruption through its unsettling of the divide between buying and gifting, commodities and art, desire and disillusionment, and creative play and a metaphysics of destruction.",
