      "abstract": "Empire of Objects will become a touchstone for future generations of Iurii Trifonov scholars. Moving well beyond what he identifies as the traditional scholarly focus on Trifonov\u2019s late novels, and especially The House on the Embankment , Benjamin M. Sutcliffe offers a concise account of Trifonov\u2019s whole oeuvre and charts compelling connections between the author\u2019s early and late writings. Sutcliffe identifies a cluster of thematic concerns that are as integral to Trifonov\u2019s debut novel, Students (1950), which earned him a Stalin prize, as they are to The House on the Embankment (1976) and other late novels known for their critique of Stalinism. These concerns include the human body, consumption, and sincerity, which Sutcliffe suggests are closely connected to one another as well as to memory, trauma, and history in Trifonov\u2019s works. In his introductory chapter, Sutcliffe argues convincingly that Trifonov\u2019s varied explorations of these themes reveal his lifelong attunement to tensions between material and immaterial facets of social life in the Soviet Union as well as his opposition to official Soviet narratives of historical progress. Given the crucial importance of each of these themes and tensions within Soviet literature and culture, readers approaching Empire of Objects with the hope of learning about more than just Trifonov will not be disappointed. At the heart of this book is the paradoxical status of materiality in the Soviet Union: against the backdrop of the Russian intelligentsia\u2019s self-avowed hostility to material values, there arose a Marxist-Leninist and later a Stalinist insistence on the importance of material bodies and goods as both the building blocks and the showpieces of the new social order. According to Sutcliffe, the complex moral valence of bodies and things amidst state-driven efforts",
